WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.402
Kubernetes deployments often come from
manifests, Helm charts,

2
00:00:06.482 --> 00:00:12.061
Kustomize overlays, generated files, or
release artifacts.

3
00:00:12.141 --> 00:00:19.438
The source may be reviewed, but the
rendered output is what the cluster receives.

4
00:00:19.518 --> 00:00:25.097
A values file, overlay, environment
setting, chart default,

5
00:00:25.177 --> 00:00:32.581
or generation step can change the
effective resources in ways that are not obvious

6
00:00:32.661 --> 00:00:35.344
from the base manifest alone.

7
00:00:35.424 --> 00:00:39.716
Rendered output can affect security
behavior.

8
00:00:39.796 --> 00:00:45.483
It can change images, labels, annotations,
service accounts,

9
00:00:45.563 --> 00:00:52.859
network exposure, volume mounts, resource
limits, security contexts, probes,

10
00:00:52.939 --> 00:00:55.515
and environment variables.

11
00:00:55.595 --> 00:01:02.891
A small values change can alter which
account a workload uses or whether a service

12
00:01:02.971 --> 00:01:04.795
becomes reachable.

13
00:01:04.875 --> 00:01:12.708
Deployment scripts should therefore make
effective output visible where practical,

14
00:01:12.788 --> 00:01:16.544
especially before high-impact applies.

15
00:01:16.624 --> 00:01:21.774
Diff and review workflows help connect
intent to action.

16
00:01:21.854 --> 00:01:29.151
A script can render the effective
manifest, compare intended resource changes,

17
00:01:29.231 --> 00:01:34.703
and require review or approval for
sensitive environments.

18
00:01:34.783 --> 00:01:37.358
This reduces blind applies,

19
00:01:37.438 --> 00:01:44.842
where generated output is sent to the
cluster without anyone seeing what changed.

20
00:01:44.922 --> 00:01:46.961
Diffs are not perfect,

21
00:01:47.041 --> 00:01:53.372
but they help operators and reviewers
reason about the actual change.

22
00:01:53.452 --> 00:01:58.602
Versioned deployment artifacts improve
traceability.

23
00:01:58.682 --> 00:02:05.120
A release should connect the approved
source, chart, values, overlay,

24
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:11.531
image reference, and rendered output where
the workflow supports it.

25
00:02:11.611 --> 00:02:17.835
That connection helps answer what was
deployed, why it was approved,

26
00:02:17.915 --> 00:02:21.885
and how to reproduce or roll back the
change.

27
00:02:21.965 --> 00:02:29.690
Secure deployment scripting makes that
chain visible instead of relying on memory or

28
00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:31.380
ad hoc job output.
